{
  "image_name": "a1fc5ca3358740057c6b93681742e7a5.png",
  "prompt_text": "This figure is a conceptual overview of nutrient pools and biogeochemical cycling in an aquatic ecosystem, linking the water column, fish, primary producers, sediments, and the atmosphere. Carbon, nitrogen, and phosphorus are shown moving among dissolved and particulate forms, with fish and aquatic plants acting as key biological reservoirs.\n\nThe main message is that organisms transform and redistribute nutrients while physical processes move them between compartments. Fish take up nutrients through grazing, then return them to the dissolved pool through excretion and respiration, and to particulate material through feces and egestion. Primary producers absorb dissolved nutrients and contribute organic matter that can slough off, sink, or be resuspended. Particulate and dissolved organic matter also accumulate in active sediments.\n\nSediments appear to be an important processing zone, where remineralization converts organic matter back into dissolved inorganic forms. Nitrogen cycling includes fixation, nitrification, and denitrification, producing exchanges involving N₂ and N₂O. Carbon cycling includes dissolved inorganic carbon and methane, with methanogenesis and methanotrophy connecting sediments, water, and air. The figure also shows diffusive exchange with the atmosphere and long-term burial in deeper sediments, while fishing extracts nutrients from the system through harvest.",
